%[text] %[text:anchor:H_936B7AC0] ### Implicit Methods
%[text] As with numerical methods for ordinary differential equations, implicit solution methods for partial differential equations require solving systems of equations. Taking the same [1D heat equation $(\\mathcal{H})${"altText":"(H)"}](internal:M_15B9D537) and the [same discretization](internal:M_5DF74724) of the variables, as with the explicit method example, we can begin by discretizing the equation in a different way..
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_872D3516] #### Step 2: Discretizing the equation using approximations of the derivatives
%[text] Rather than a forward difference approximation for $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}${"altText":"(partial u)\/(partial t)"}, in this case, use a first-order backward difference equation,
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t}(t\_j,x\_m) \\approx \\frac{u(t\_j,x\_m)-u(t\_j-\\Delta t,x\_m)}{\\Delta t} = \\frac{u\_{j,m}-u\_{j-1,m}}{\\Delta t}${"altText":"(partial u)\/(partial t)(t\_j,x\_m) approximately (u(t\_j,x\_m)-u(t\_j- Delta t,x\_m))\/(Delta t) = (u\_{j,m}-u\_{j-1,m})\/(Delta t)"}, 
%[text] on mesh points. For the spatial derivative, let's use the same [second-order centered difference approximation](internal:M_915E8BEB) that we did in the explicit method example. Shifting values from $j${"altText":"j"} to $j+1${"altText":"j+1"} so we don't try to refer to $u\_{-1}${"altText":"u\_{-1}"} when $j=0${"altText":"j=0"}, and plugging these into the differential equation, we find:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\frac{u\_{j+1,m}-u\_{j,m}}{\\Delta t} = \\gamma\\left(\\frac{u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1}}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\right)${"altText":"(u\_{j+1,m}-u\_{j,m})\/(Delta t) = gamma ((u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1})\/(( Delta x)^2))"}, 
%[text] which simplifies to
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_FF1F54C7] $u\_{j+1,m} -  \\frac{\\gamma \\Delta t}{(\\Delta x)^2}\\left(u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1}\\right)=  u\_{j,m}${"altText":"u\_{j+1,m} - (gamma Delta t)\/(( Delta x)^2)(u\_{j+1,m+1}-2u\_{j+1,m}+u\_{j+1,m-1})= u\_{j,m}"}.   $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7223CAA7] #### Step 3: Check the stability of this method
%[text] ![](text:image:03bd) **Exercise 2:** 
%[text] Follow the example of the [Fourier stability analysis of the explicit method](internal:H_0BA93E0C) to compute the Fourier stability of the implicit method $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}. 
%[text] **Solution:** 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_951940D5] #### Step 4: Formulate the linear algebra problem 
%[text] Since we have chosen to use the same discretization of the problem, we can use the same vector $\\vec{u}^{(j)} = \\pmatrix{u\_{j,1}\\cr \\vdots \\cr u\_{j,n-1}}${"altText":"vector(u)^{(j)} = matrix \[u\_{j,1}; ...; u\_{j,n-1}\]"}. In terms of $\\vec{u}^{(j+1)}${"altText":"vector(u)^{(j+1)}"} and $\\vec{u}^{(j)}${"altText":"vector(u)^{(j)}"}, the entire [system of equations $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}](internal:M_FF1F54C7) relating times $t\_j${"altText":"t\_j"} and $t\_{j+1}${"altText":"t\_{j+1}"} is given by 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $B\\vec{u}^{(j+1)} - \\vec{b}^{(j+1)} = \\vec{u}^{(j)}${"altText":"B vector(u)^{(j+1)} - vector(b)^{(j+1)} = vector(u)^{(j)}"},
%[text] or in other words, 
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_56AE4F3A] $\\vec{u}^{(j+1)} = B^{-1}\\left(\\vec{u}^{(j)}+\\vec{b}^{(j+1)}\\right)${"altText":"vector(u)^{(j+1)} = B^{-1}(vector(u)^{(j)}+vector(b)^{(j+1)})"},
%[text] where $B = \\pmatrix{1+2\\mu & -\\mu & & & &  \\cr -\\mu & 1+2\\mu & -\\mu & && \\cr & -\\mu & 1+2\\mu & -\\mu && \\cr && -\\mu&\\ddots & \\ddots & \\cr &&& \\ddots & \\ddots & -\\mu \\cr &&&& -\\mu & 1+2\\mu}${"altText":"B = matrix \[1+2 mu, - mu, 0, 0, 0, 0; - mu, 1+2 mu, - mu, 0, 0, 0; 0, - mu, 1+2 mu, - mu, 0, 0; 0, 0, - mu, diagonal continuation, diagonal continuation, 0; 0, 0, 0, diagonal continuation, diagonal continuation, - mu; 0, 0, 0, 0, - mu, 1+2 mu\]"} and $\\vec{b}^{(j+1)} = \\pmatrix{\\mu\\alpha\_{j+1}\\cr 0 \\cr 0 \\cr \\vdots \\cr 0 \\cr \\mu\\beta\_{j+1}}${"altText":"vector(b)^{(j+1)} = matrix \[mu alpha \_{j+1}; 0; 0; ...; 0; mu beta \_{j+1}\]"}. 
%[text] 
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7BA80554] #### Step 5: How good is our estimate?
%[text] ![](text:image:431f) **Exercise 3**
%[text] Follow the [error calculation](internal:H_72D14D00) in the explicit method example to determine an appropriate relationship between `dt` and `dx` to control the derivative approximation error.
% Enter the correct relationship here
dt = dx;

%%
%[text] ![](text:image:28de) **Exercise 4**
%[text] Implement the [implicit method $(\\mathcal{I})${"altText":"(I)"}](internal:M_56AE4F3A). 
open("implicitPDE.m")
%%
%[text] Test your function by solving the same problem as the explicit method.
u = implicitPDE(f,alpha,beta,tEnd,dx,gamma,L);
%[text] Plot the initial value `u0` and the final value `u` on the same plot.
plot(0:dx:L,f(0:dx:L))
hold on
plot(0:dx:L,[alpha(tEnd);u;beta(tEnd)])
hold off
title("Solving $\frac{\partial u}{\partial t} = \gamma \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}$","Interpreter","latex")
subtitle("$t=$"+tEnd,"Interpreter","latex")
xlabel("$x$","Interpreter","latex")
ylabel("$u$","Interpreter","latex")
legend(["u(0,x)","u(t,x)"],"Location","north")
